body:
## Description of Issue
Using the AceMind simulator for Fanduel MLB, I noticed that my final secondary stack allocations over all generated lineups was not close to the percentages I had set. Reviewing the code it appears this could be caused by the `total_estimated_cost` variable being estimated too low and thus not adding the secondary stacks to the `secondary_lens` and `secondary_teams` variables. (See code below for reference)

https://github.com/chanzer0/MLB-DFS-Tools/blob/0be2275e6d9feeecccc6131dfccd2fda9114fdcc/src/mlb_gpp_simulator.py#L1188-L1221

## Steps to Reproduce
From AceMind's default FanDuel MLB Simulator settings change the following variables:

- % Field Using Stacks -> 100
- % Field w/ Max Stack L -> 100
- Secondary Stack % -> 100

Upon completion of simulation, download all lineups that were generated and using Excel or Python compare the percentage of lineups with 4/4, 4/3 stacks to what was expected (about 50/50 split).

Repeat the same process, this time lowering the min salary to about 30,000 and see how that changes outputs.


## Test Cases
I was testing with Fanduel lineups, so some of the variables that are consistent across tests are: `num_hitters_per_lineup = 8`, `num_pitchers = 1`, `len(self.roster_construction = 9`. Some abbrevations used in the table below are Primary Team Average Salary (PTAS), Secondary Team Average Salary (STAS), Average Pitcher Salary (APS), Average Hitter Salary (AHS).

